<<<
+ brazen, buxom, crooked, equal, can take
===
+ *brazen, buxom, crooked, equal,* can take
>>>

<<<
8. Adverbs not formed with -ly from adjectives
===
3. Adverbs not formed with *-ly* from adjectives
>>>

<<<
in form with them, use -er & -est naturally (runs faster, sleeps sounder, hits hardest, hold it tighter); some independent adverbs, as soon, often, seldom, do the same; -ly adverbs, though comparatives in -lier are possible in archaic & poetic style (wiselier said, softlier nurtured), now prefer more wisely &c. ;
===
in form with them, use *-er & -est* naturally (*runs faster, sleeps sounder, hits hardest, hold it tighter*); some independent adverbs, as *soon, often, seldom,* do the same; *-ly* adverbs, though comparatives in *-lier* are possible in archaic & poetic style (*wiselier said, softlier nurtured*), now prefer *more wisely* &c.;
>>>

<<<
as an adverb (easier said than done; he writes cleverer than he talks; try to state your case clearer); this, however
===
as an adverb (*easier said than done; he writes cleverer than he talks; try to state your case clearer*); this, however
>>>

<<<
ordinary use, i.e. without the stylistic
===
ordinary use, i. e. without the stylistic
>>>

<<<
of forming a superlative in -est, used with the & serving as an emphatic form simply, while no-one would think of making a comparative in -er from them: in the brutalest, civilest, timidest, winningest, cogentest, cheerfullest, cunningest, doggedest, drunkenest, candidest, damnablest, manner.
===
of forming a superlative in *-est,* used with *the* & serving as an emphatic form simply, while no-one would think of making a comparative in *-er* from them: *in the brutalest, civilest, timidest, winningest, cogentest, cheerfullest, cunningest, doggedest, drunkenest, candidest, damnablest, manner.*
>>>

<<<
most invite this treatment are -ful, -ing, -able, -ed, & -id; on the other hand the very common adjective terminations -tve, -ic, & -ous, reject it altogether (curtouser & curiouser is a product of Wonderland).
===
most invite this treatment are *-ful, -ing, -able, -ed, & -id*; on the other hand the very common adjective terminations *-ive, -ic, & -ous,* reject it altogether (*curiouser & curiouser* is a product of Wonderland).
>>>

<<<
5. As a stylistic device, based on NOVELTY-HUNTING, & developing into disagreeable MANNERISM, the use of -er & -est is extended to many adjectives normally taking more &
===
5. As a stylistic device, based on ^^NOVELTY-HUNTING^^, & developing into disagreeable ^^MANNERISM^^, the use of *-er & -est* is extended to many adjectives normally taking *more* &
>>>

<<<
+ most, & the reader gets pulled up at intervals by beautifuller, delicater, ancientest, diligentest, delectablest, dolefuller, devotedest, admirablest, & the like.
===
+ *most,* & the reader gets pulled up at intervals by *beautifuller, delicater, ancientest, diligentest, delectablest, dolefuller, devotedest, admirablest,* & the like.
>>>

<<<
6. The emotional -est without the. Mile Nau, an actress of considerable technical skill & a valuable power of exhibiting deepest emotion; this sentence
===
6. The emotional *-est* without *the.* *Mlle Nau, an actress of considerable technical skill & a valuable power of exhibiting deepest emotion*; this sentence
>>>

